 "productionNotes": "Every source consulted agrees on the shape of the number and none agrees on the number itself. Sports Car Market's profile gives 198,000 Spiders. Hagerty and restore-an-old-car.com both say nearly 200,000, and Wikipedia says almost 200,000. Wikipedia also carries a year-by-year table for 1970 through 1985 that totals 172,818, which leaves the 1966-1969 cars uncounted and so cannot be compared directly with the lifetime figures. The US share is where the sources genuinely part company. Wikipedia states that 75 percent of production went to the US market, which on its own 200,000 figure would be about 150,000 cars. restore-an-old-car.com says over 170,000 were sold to North American markets, which would be closer to 85 percent and includes Canada. Neither cites a primary record that this research could reach, and no factory or club production ledger was fetchable, so no total and no US count is stated as a fact on this page. The late cars are better documented by year: Wikipedia's table shows 4,747 built in 1981, 3,456 in 1982, 2,480 in 1983, 2,577 in 1984 and 1,504 in 1985, and restore-an-old-car.com separately reports about 1,500 cars for the final model year, which is consistent. The special variants carry their own disputes. The US Turbo Spider is put at about 700 cars by Wikipedia, Hagerty, restore-an-old-car.com and Barn Finds, while Driven to Write notes Fiat of North America projected about 1,000 a year and that how many were actually sold remains unclear. The Abarth Rally homologation car is 1,013 cars per Hagerty and fewer than 1,000 per Wikipedia; Hagerty adds that very few exist in the US, and no source consulted documents it being sold new here.",
